#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088991 :tutorial/content "The **standard form** equation of a line is\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b$, and $c$ are all integers,\n- $a \\geq 0$, and\n- $a,b$, and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n$\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}$\nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$, $-1$, and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1$. Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4$, and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2$.\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." :tutorial/id #uuid "739ff387-5940-547f-adac-8b2ac8e5a229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9126 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088993 :tutorial/content "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x - 1 \\\\\ny - 2x &= -1 \\\\\n- 2x + y &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nHowever, it violates the rule\n\n$$\na \\geq 0\n$$\n\nWe can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= -1 \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-2x + y) &= (-1) \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n2x - y &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b$, and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and\n\n$$\na \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1$." :tutorial/id #uuid "914fb469-510e-5e74-92bc-6174870cbd47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9221 :tutorial/title "Converting a Line to Standard Form"}]}
